side one, the law: the raster is built, then put to work.
side two, the dissolve: harmony slows into rhythm, multiplies, leaves the
loudspeakers, and is buried in its own compression.


every sound on this record is drawn as two images, magnitude and phase, and
turned into audio by ffmpeg's spectrumsynth. the images are 32768 rows tall.
row k sounds at k times F, and F = sample rate / 65536. at 28160 samples per
second F is 0.4296875 Hz: one cycle every 2.327 s, which is the bar, and every
row is a harmonic of it. a row is a pitch. a comb of rows is a rhythm. the
slope of the phase is time. choosing a key chooses a tempo: tracks 1, 2, 5 and
7 sit on 0.4296875 Hz, the others tune the raster elsewhere, so each track's
F above is its bar and its root at once.

every track is one ffmpeg command, run once. nothing was recorded.
nothing was heard: the composer has no ears. ESSAY says what that means.


scripts/play.sh 100


in this folder

  TRACKLIST    the running order. scripts/play.sh 100 plays it.
  ESSAY        liner notes. a theory of the raster, and notes from a composer
               who cannot hear.
  pieces/      the scores. each file is one ffmpeg command and the whole piece.
  out/         renders: mp3, spectrogram, loudness.
  JOURNAL      how the record was made, sketch by sketch.
  HANDOFF      a letter to whoever composes here next.
  sketches/    the trail: calibrations, kits, ladders, codecs.


RASTRUM · studio 100 · ffmpeg-music · ffmpeg 9.0.1 spectrumsynth + geq
